  "evidence": "A: NHS waits and capacity (40,000 extra weekly appointments, double CT/MRI, more GPs and dentist rescue), housing supply and planning reform (1.5m homes, targets, new towns), cost of living/economy (strict fiscal rules, no rises in NI/Income Tax/VAT, industrial strategy and infrastructure), crime (Neighbourhood Policing Guarantee), and borders (Border Security Command, clear asylum backlog). B: Tax cuts (halve employee NI, abolish main self‑employed NI), migration clampdown (annual cap, monthly Rwanda flights), NHS investment (funding above inflation, 92,000 nurses, 28,000 doctors, £3.4bn tech), housing (1.6m homes, Help to Buy), and National Service at 18.",
  "reasoning": "Because voters’ top concerns centered on the NHS, the economy/cost of living and housing, A foregrounds cutting NHS waits with concrete capacity boosts, maintains tax stability for working people while focusing on growth, and commits to a large housebuilding and planning reform push. B addresses these too but places greater emphasis on migration controls, tax cuts and National Service, which are less central to the broad priority mix, so A better mirrors what these voters cared about.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "A"
}
